# ZETA RAYS — protection against out-of-memory freezes.
# Without it, when RAM runs out the kernel can take minutes to react and the
# computer looks frozen. earlyoom steps in earlier, stopping the program that
# is using the most memory.
#   -m 5  acts below 5% free RAM
#   -s 10 and below 10% free swap
#   -r 0  no periodic report in the logs
# The desktop must be protected (--avoid): stopping it would lose the session.
# Heavy programs that can be restarted are stopped first (--prefer).
# Note: systemd does not interpret quotes inside this variable, so the
# expressions must not contain spaces or quotes.
# The names are those of the actual process: scripts run as "python3" or
# "bash", so they are not listed here (the memory limits of their respective
# services already cover them).
# -m 12: act when less than 12% of memory is free.
# At 5% the computer has already been struggling for a while — the disk is
# thrashing, windows do not respond — and acting there means acting after
# the user has already seen the freeze. Better earlier and silently.
# --avoid: the desktop components are never touched, otherwise the cure
# would be worse than the disease. --prefer: the big memory hogs that can be
# restarted without losing anything are sacrificed first.
EARLYOOM_ARGS=-m 12 -s 20 -r 0 --avoid ^(Hyprland|waybar|mako|swaybg|sddm|systemd|dbus-daemon|zeta-emergency)$ --prefer ^(ollama|llama-server|tesseract|pdftotext|firefox-esr)$
